The problem involves two cars traveling on a highway at constant speeds. The first car is traveling at 60 miles per hour, and the second car passes it at 70 miles per hour. The key is to determine the distance between them after 2 hours, starting from the moment the second car passes the first.

Since the second car passes the first at the same point in time, we can consider this moment as time zero. From here, the second car is moving faster than the first, so the distance between them increases over time. The relative speed between the two cars is the difference in their speeds:

$$
70 \text{ mph} - 60 \text{ mph} = 10 \text{ mph}
$$

This relative speed means the distance between the two cars increases by 10 miles every hour. After 2 hours, the distance between them will be:

$$
10 \text{ mph} \times 2 \text{ hours} = 20 \text{ miles}
$$

Thus, the distance between the two cars after 2 hours is $\boxed{20}$ miles. 

