The problem involves calculating the total cost of Jello needed to make enough cups for a birthday party. 

### Step 1: Calculate Total Jello Cups Needed
Greg wants to provide 4 jello cups per child for 30 kids:
$$
30 \text{ kids} \times 4 \text{ cups per child} = 120 \text{ cups}
$$

### Step 2: Determine Number of Boxes Needed
Each three-ounce box makes 10 cups. To find the number of boxes required:
$$
\frac{120 \text{ cups}}{10 \text{ cups per box}} = 12 \text{ boxes}
$$

### Step 3: Calculate Total Cost
Each box costs $1.25. For 12 boxes:
$$
12 \text{ boxes} \times \$1.25 \text{ per box} = \$15.00
$$

Thus, the total cost of Jello is:
$$
\boxed{15}
$$
